#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134851} :activity/id #uuid "cc83e826-b428-5261-8109-f36f4450dc4f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051000 :topic/id #uuid "789238e2-4251-5080-bbc3-e713bd84a64b" :topic/math-academy-id 2109} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134851 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135122 :tutorial/content "Until now, we've been dealing with line integrals with respect to arc length. In this lesson, we will learn about other types of line integral. \n\nSuppose that $C$ is a path in the $xy$-plane with initial point $A$ and terminal point $B.$ The line integral of the function $f(x,y)$ **with respect to $\\boldsymbol x$** along the path $C$ is denoted\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTo understand what this integral represents, we imagine traversing $C$ from $A$ to $B.$ At each point $P$ that lies on $C,$ we project the point on $f$ that lies directly above $P$ onto the plane $y=0.$ Doing this for every point on $C$ traces out a curve. The line integral of $f$ with respect to $x$ gives the (signed) area bounded by this curve and the $x$-axis.\n\nThis integral represents the total area accumulated when [math]f[/math] is projected on the plane [math]y=0[/math] as [math]C[/math] is traversed from the initial point [math]A[/math] to the terminal point [math]B.[/math] \n\n![](images/62/62c07a36cbf79459021ee7be125536a8f9c1bc3c193a99eabfcd3fc851b8eb0c.png)\n\nTo calculate a line integral with respect to $x,$ we must first parameterize $C$ using a parameter $t\\in [a,b].$ Then, we apply the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}x  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's take a look at a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "2f1254b7-43b2-5b19-b5e9-cf33ff6cffe8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5812 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4a502006-31bd-5b97-b374-0fc7fe27b6e6" :step/math-academy-id 35614 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134852}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134852 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133891 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133707 :question/id #uuid "bbd835f9-a428-558c-9db7-838d02267a0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9512"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c243e957-272a-5bde-8878-7264a94e447e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050616}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387477}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to X"} :step/id #uuid "836166ec-b6a4-5203-a460-a9fbed3d6962" :step/math-academy-id 35615 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134855}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134853 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133892 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133708 :question/id #uuid "7417f4d8-262c-5d16-9b35-f1c9710345e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9513"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0712bb6-c0f8-5551-8362-ae23629aa618" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387498}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to Y"} :step/id #uuid "3c7d4502-6f95-54db-ae3c-2f1adac21e09" :step/math-academy-id 35616 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134854}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133893 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133709 :question/id #uuid "e45bef0c-86ba-5030-bb0c-274df22673f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9521"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369272}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"} :step/id #uuid "10fbb7b7-ba3c-5cff-a825-8a6c4e82bc79" :step/math-academy-id 35642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135123 :tutorial/content "The **line integral with respect to** $\\boldsymbol y$ of the function $f(x,y)$ along the path $C$ is denoted\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nThis integral represents the total area accumulated when $f$ is projected on the plane $x=0$ as $C$ is traversed from the initial point $A$ to the terminal point $B.$ \n\n![](images/9c/9ccbabd17ff18511ed26e8098b2fad41104bbd921a32eb639ae1a74bcab6f94b.png)\n\nTo calculate a line integral with respect to $y,$ we must first parameterize $C$ using a parameter $t\\in [a,b].$ Then, we apply the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}y  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e58d5cab-69bb-507b-a225-b621efc9b93f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5816 :tutorial/title "Line Integrals With Respect to Y"} :step/id #uuid "452fc7d7-48f9-5478-a20c-0ad745c8bca0" :step/math-academy-id 35670 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134853}}] :activity/title "Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133995}]}
